# Rubric Criterion Issues in an RLHE Annotation Pipeline 
Technical Analysis: Incomplete Atomicity, Reward Signal Distortion, and the Case for a "Not COMPLETE" Rubric Issue Category RLHE Annotation Quality 15 Scored Examples (A-O) + Post-Analysis

# 1. Background 

## 1.1. Original task as given 

### 1.1.1 Quick issue statement, which be further explained

This task runs into a rule criterion issue that does not fit any current "Rule Problems/Issues" classification options; Rule 1 (specified in the [Simplified](#Simplified-prompt-and-rules-for-analysis) 
section) is **incomplete and thus not atomic**, because it **leaves out a check that 
should be bundled**. That relevant check is in Rule 2 (also specified in the 
[Simplified](#Simplified-prompt-and-rules-for-analysis) section,) where we already see 
problems with a global check and the local check that should be in Rule 1 leading to 
mis-signaling the Reinforcement Learning reward function. Many more mis-signaling problems follow, especially those that are caused by an incompleteness in the "Rule Problems/Issues" classification list, which leave out an important part of the potential rule response space. Since the scoring (policy-gradient-based scoring for teaching an AI to write good rules) takes into account not only the fix as it is reported, but the _type_ of fix that is reported, the reward signal becomes especially distorted by the lack of a logically necessary classification for the "Rule Problems/Issues", which will hereafter also be referred to as RPIs.

### 1.1.2. Complete Prompt Description
 
Though I use a simplified example for the analysis, it's useful to see the complete 
task. 

This is a **multi-turn prompt**. Note that the response doesn't follow all elements of the rules as given by the user/system.

Also note that the requirement to use https://markdownlivepreview.com/ in reporting leads to strange quotation marks up front, as well as the extra `<span>` tags that you'll see if you're looking at unrendered text.

